class:: PMNetworkOsc
summary:: Three-operator phase- and through-zero frequency-modulation network
categories:: UGens>Generators>Deterministic
related:: Guides/NovelOscillators

description::

PMNetworkOsc contains three oscillators connected by independent phase- and
linear-frequency-modulation matrices. Every operator is returned separately.
Feedback uses the previous oversampled substep, so cyclic networks have a
defined one-substep delay and remain deterministic.

Matrices are flattened in row-major order. Entry
code::(row * 3) + column:: routes the preceding output of operator
code::column:: into operator code::row::. code::pm:: coefficients are radians;
code::fm:: coefficients are hertz. Ratios, matrices, and amplitudes may contain
audio-rate signals.

The closest vanilla UGen is link::Classes/SinOscFB::. Both define feedback
without an algebraic loop, but PMNetworkOsc has three independently returned
operators, arbitrary routing, simultaneous PM and through-zero FM, four
waveforms, and optional oversampling.

classmethods::

method:: ar

argument:: freq
Signed network base frequency in hertz.

argument:: ratios
Exactly three operator-frequency ratios. Elements may be audio rate.

argument:: pm
Exactly nine row-major PM coefficients in radians. Elements may be audio rate.

argument:: fm
Exactly nine row-major linear-FM coefficients in hertz. Elements may be audio
rate.

argument:: amplitudes
Exactly three operator output amplitudes, clamped to code::-4..4::.

argument:: waveforms
Exactly three initialization-rate shape indices: code::0:: sine, code::1::
triangle, code::2:: saw, and code::3:: pulse.

argument:: oversample
Initialization-rate value code::1::, code::2::, or code::4::.

argument:: mul
Multiplier applied to all three outputs.

argument:: add
Offset added to all three outputs after multiplication.

returns::
An array containing operators 0, 1, and 2.

method:: kr

PMNetworkOsc is audio-rate only. This method throws an error.

subsection:: Discussion

A zero matrix produces three independent oscillators. PM changes only readout
phase; FM changes the signed phase increment and may reverse it through zero.
Strong cyclic coefficients can be chaotic but non-finite internal results are
replaced with zero and output is conservatively bounded.

examples::

code::
(
{ PMNetworkOsc.ar(110)[0] * 0.075 }.play;
)
::

code::
(
{
    var pm = [
        0, 2.4, 0,
        0, 0, 1.2,
        0.7, 0, 0
    ];
    var fm = [
        0, 0, 0,
        0, 0, 45,
        20, 0, 0
    ];
    Splay.ar(
        PMNetworkOsc.ar(
            70, [1, 1.5, 2], pm, fm,
            [1, 0.5, 0.3], [0, 1, 2], 4
        )
    ) * 0.04;
}.play;
)
::

